--TEST--
A `fn` literal's optional self-name calls the closure it names, from that closure's own body and nowhere else — `rule:types/anonymous-function-self-name`, `rule:types/callable-values`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3's worked example: an expression body calling itself. The name is
// visible inside the body only, so nothing outside this line can reach it.
callable $fact = fn fact(int $n): int => $n <= 1 ? 1 : $n * fact($n - 1);
echo $fact(5), "\n";

// The same with a block body. § 3 composes with both of § 1's shapes rather
// than being a third closure form.
callable $fib = fn fib(int $n): int => {
    if ($n <= 1) {
        return $n;
    }
    return fib($n - 1) + fib($n - 2);
};
echo $fib(10), "\n";

// The self-name is not the variable the closure was assigned to: § 3 makes it
// lexical, resolved against the literal itself. So reassigning that variable
// leaves the recursion alone — which is the whole difference between this and
// a local the body happened to capture.
callable $g = fn twice(int $n): int => $n <= 1 ? 1 : 2 * twice($n - 1);
callable $keep = $g;
$g = fn (int $n): int => 999;
echo $keep(5), "\n";
echo $g(5), "\n";

// A capture sits beside the self-name without either becoming the other: the
// name is not in the captured set (§ 3), `$step` is.
int $step = 2;
callable $count = fn down(int $n): int => $n <= 0 ? 0 : 1 + down($n - $step);
echo $count(10), "\n";

// The declared return type is what a *self-call* answers with, and that is
// what makes `$n + sum(...)` an `int` sum rather than an unchecked one: § 4's
// opaque `callable` describes the value, and the self-name is not a value of
// it. Called through the variable instead, the same closure is `mixed` — so
// the difference is which of the two spellings is written, not which closure.
callable $sum = fn sum(int $n): int => $n <= 0 ? 0 : $n + sum($n - 1);
echo $sum(4), "\n";
--EXPECT--
120
55
16
999
5
10
